{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.98,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly answers the query ('main kinds of issues') with a rich, well-distributed taxonomy that supports grouping and comparison. Sampled values are sound readings: outage, feature_request, and billing_issue all match their source text. The invoice duplicate row arguably could be billing but is labeled billing_issue correctly; the CSV BOM row labeled bug_functional is defensible. Minor imperfection is that categories are somewhat inferred, but readings are warranted."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_severity",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Provides a genuine new axis (impact tiering) useful for prioritizing/segmenting issue types, and is not merely redundant with the original 'priority' column since it is derived from description content. Sampled values are mostly warranted: the outage blocking a board presentation as blocking_p1 and the pin-reports enhancement as low_impact are well grounded. Some calls are subjective (session-drop as medium) but reasonable."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_scope",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.72,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Scope (single user vs tenant-wide vs subset) is a genuine analytical axis and uses an appropriate 'not_present' for silent rows. However some values overreach: the CSV/BOM row is marked entire_tenant though the text only describes the customer's own pipeline and does not state tenant-wide impact; the billing invoice row as single_user is defensible. The outage 'all members of the Acme tenant' -> entire_tenant is well grounded. One unsupported confident specific lowers groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "actionability",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A useful action-routing axis (engineering fix vs roadmap vs self-service) that helps operationalize the issue-kind analysis. Several sampled values are grounded (feature_request -> product_roadmap, outage -> requires_engineering_fix). But the billing invoice row is labeled requires_data_recovery, which the text does not support -- it is a corrected-invoice/billing correction, not data recovery. This is an unwarranted specific that lowers groundedness."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
  "source_groundedness": 0.8,
  "rationale": "All four columns give distinct, non-redundant axes (category, severity, scope, actionability) that directly serve the query about kinds of support issues and enable grouping/comparison. issue_category is the strongest and most on-point. The set is generally well grounded, with correct use of 'not_present' for silent rows, but affected_scope and actionability each contain at least one over-confident value not supported by the source text (entire_tenant for a single-customer pipeline issue; requires_data_recovery for a billing correction), producing modest hallucination that caps groundedness."
}
